@page "/installation"

<PageTitle>Installation | Spillgebees.Blazor.Map</PageTitle>

<h1>Installation</h1>

<h2>NuGet Package</h2>

<p>Add the package to your project:</p>

<CodeBlock Language="bash" Files="@(new Dictionary<string, string>
           {
               ["Terminal"] = "dotnet add package Spillgebees.Blazor.Map",
           })" />

<h2>Stylesheet</h2>

<p>
    Load the stylesheet. It bundles the MapLibre GL CSS and the
    styles for the library's controls and popups. How you reference it depends on the hosting model:
</p>

<CodeBlock Language="html" Files="@_stylesheetUsage" />

<p>
    In Blazor Web Apps and Blazor Server apps on .NET 9 or later, <code>@@Assets["…"]</code> resolves the
    fingerprinted asset URL produced by
    <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/fundamentals/static-files" target="_blank"
       rel="noopener">Map Static Assets</a> (<code>MapStaticAssets()</code>, on by default
    in the templates), so the stylesheet is compressed and cache-busted on every release.
</p>

<p>
    Standalone Blazor WebAssembly apps use the plain path. Note that the .NET 10
    <code>#[.{fingerprint}]</code> placeholders in <code>index.html</code>
    (<code>OverrideHtmlAssetPlaceholders</code>) currently apply to scripts only — stylesheets are not
    fingerprinted, so the plain path is the correct form there.
</p>

<h2>JavaScript Module</h2>

<p>
    No script tags are required. The library ships with a <code>lib.module.js</code> file that Blazor
    loads automatically via the standard <a
    href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/fundamentals/startup#javascript-initializers"
    target="_blank" rel="noopener">JS initializers</a> convention.
    The MapLibre GL JS bundle is included, so no CDN reference is needed.
</p>

<h2>Minimal Usage</h2>

<p>
    Add the component to any Razor page or component. A fixed height is required for the map to render.
</p>

<CodeBlock Language="razor" Files="@_minimalUsage" />

<h2>Changing the Map Style</h2>

<p>
    When no style is specified, the map defaults to <strong>OpenFreeMap Liberty</strong>, a clean vector style
    with no API key required. To use a different style, pass it via the <code>Style</code> parameter:
</p>

<CodeBlock Language="razor" Files="@_styleUsage" />

@code {
    private static readonly Dictionary<string, string> _stylesheetUsage = new()
    {
        ["App.razor (Blazor Web App / Server)"] = """
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/Spillgebees.Blazor.Map/Spillgebees.Blazor.Map.lib.module.css"]" />
            """,
        ["index.html (standalone WebAssembly)"] = """
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="_content/Spillgebees.Blazor.Map/Spillgebees.Blazor.Map.lib.module.css" />
            """,
    };

    private static readonly Dictionary<string, string> _minimalUsage = new()
    {
        ["MyPage.razor"] = """
            @using Spillgebees.Blazor.Map

            <SgbMap Center="@(new Coordinate(51.505, -0.09))" Zoom="13" Height="500px" />
            """,
    };

    private static readonly Dictionary<string, string> _styleUsage = new()
    {
        ["MyPage.razor"] = """
            <SgbMap Center="@(new Coordinate(51.505, -0.09))"
                    Zoom="13"
                    Style="@(MapStyle.OpenFreeMap.Bright)"
                    Height="500px" />
            """,
    };
}

<p>
    Other built-in presets are <code>MapStyle.OpenFreeMap.Positron</code> and
    <code>MapStyle.OpenStreetMap.Standard</code>.
    For any other source use <code>MapStyle.FromUrl</code>, <code>MapStyle.FromWmsUrl</code>,
    <code>MapStyle.FromWmtsUrl</code>, or <code>MapStyle.FromArcGisMapServer</code>.
    See the <a href="components/sgb-map#styles">Styles</a> example for more detail.
</p>
